Like maybe make something simpler that drives people to do that action that is driving retention, which in turn is driving renewal.

754
01:07:34,560 --> 01:07:37,760
So maybe simplify a little bit the app experience itself.

755
01:07:38,400 --> 01:07:47,520
In app messages, like dynamic in app messages are very efficient and eventually if they're already off the app, a push notification would be a main driver.

756
01:07:47,680 --> 01:07:55,360
But I guess my answer here is you're not going to convince people not to churn if they stop using the app.

757
01:07:55,680 --> 01:07:56,560
So that's the only way.

758
01:07:57,680 --> 01:07:59,760
Well, I don't have much to add there.

759
01:08:00,240 --> 01:08:01,040
So there's one question.

760
01:08:01,600 --> 01:08:06,720
here that I'll take because I think this is a really fun question and something I think a lot about.

761
01:08:07,440 --> 01:08:12,560
How viable is it really for solo app developers to earn a living in 2022 on the App Store?

762
01:08:12,800 --> 01:08:21,360
I've heard apps are dead, et cetera, which of course is not true, but still it seems difficult to make a living on it with such a crowded app market.

763
01:08:21,760 --> 01:08:22,800
I guess I need to learn marketing.

764
01:08:24,080 --> 01:08:26,640
So that's a fantastic question.

765
01:08:27,200 --> 01:08:36,000
And my answer is that in some ways it's easier in 2022 to make a living on apps than it ever has been.

766
01:08:36,880 --> 01:08:39,600
But there are a few key pointsints to make around this.

767
01:08:40,000 --> 01:08:52,960
One is that, and I'm not just saying this because I'm at RevenueCat, but I really do think that the subscription model has opened up the opportunity for smaller niche apps to make enough money to make a living from.

768
01:08:53,200 --> 01:08:59,920
But that's kind of the key number one is that what you need to do is like, don't go out and try to be the next headspace.

769
01:09:01,280 --> 01:09:05,680
If your goal is to be the next headspace, that's just a you're you're building a different product.

770
01:09:06,480 --> 01:09:12,320
You need to start with some level of funding because yes, you know, competing and growing is going to be brutally hard.

771
01:09:15,120 --> 01:09:23,520
and you want to make a go at being an indeed developer and making a good living on the app store, instead go the opposite direction.

772
01:09:23,760 --> 01:09:31,200
Go really, really niche, but in a niche that that people are willing to spend money and that people find valuable.

773
01:09:31,600 --> 01:09:34,640
And we've talked about this on the subclub podcast quite a bit.

774
01:09:34,720 --> 01:09:40,400
If you haven't seen that subclub dot com fishbrain, we had the CMO on recently.

775
01:09:40,800 --> 01:09:43,280
And they're actually a huge app.

776
01:09:43,600 --> 01:09:53,600
So this is not even solo developer stuff, but the fishing hobby, people spend thousands, tens of thousands of dollars on boats and trips and other stuff.

777
01:09:54,240 --> 01:09:59,120
So if you're a solo developer looking to make a living right now, go find a niche.

778
01:09:59,600 --> 01:10:02,480
One where people are going to find it valuable enough to spend money.

779
01:10:03,280 --> 01:10:08,000
And then two, where there is some form where you understand how you're going to get attention for it.

780
01:10:08,560 --> 01:10:13,040
Don't go build an app and have this if I build it, they will come kind of mentality.

781
01:10:13,760 --> 01:10:15,600
You need some level of built in marketing.

782
01:10:16,160 --> 01:10:19,040
So I think knitting apps is a really interesting example.

783
01:10:19,680 --> 01:10:21,600
There's three or four good ones.

784
01:10:22,160 --> 01:10:56,160
And there's one from this solo developer, Becky, who I don't think is making a living from it yet or anything, but it's a really interesting niche where if you can deliver enough value and if you can embed yourself in a knitting community and, you know, if you're part of a really big Reddit forum where you're not pitching your app but you know, you're asking for feedback or other things, if if you can find a community, a niche that has some level of built in marketing that people are going to get excited about it, they're going to share it on their knitting YouTube channel.

785
01:10:56,320 --> 01:10:57,440
They're going to share it on Reddit.

786
01:10:57,760 --> 01:11:04,000
They're going to tweet about it because you're just bringing so much value to this little tiny niche that's just underserved.

787
01:11:05,280 --> 01:11:10,640
If you're going to make a go at being a successful Indian in 2022, I think that's the approach.

788
01:11:11,120 --> 01:11:15,920
And yes, you absolutely need to learn marketing and think about marketing from the very beginning.

789
01:11:16,160 --> 01:11:22,480
It's that you have to know how your app is going to get attention before you go build it.

790
01:11:22,720 --> 01:11:40,640
I mean, you know, you can throw things against the wall and hope they're going to stick, but if you're going to spend six months building an app, you need to spend a month doing some market research, really thinking about how you're going to get attention, really kind of validating some of the ideas that people actually care that people are actually willing to spend money for it.

791
01:11:41,120 --> 01:11:44,160
So with that, I think that's a pretty good summary.

792
01:11:44,880 --> 01:11:45,840
Any follow-up there, Thomas?

793
01:11:46,640 --> 01:11:49,760
Yeah, no, I also believe there's still a lot of opportunity.

794
01:11:50,160 --> 01:12:03,120
And I'd say almost, almost even more, like because the big space are taken by big guys and it's not done, but yeah, you're not going to move at a pace and calm, it goes more niche, more niche and more niche.

795
01:12:03,280 --> 01:12:16,960
But then VC back company, they won't have the scale to make that niche go to the VC goal, which opens up space for maybe not solo people company, but small, small teams to actually make a really good living out of it.

796
01:12:17,920 --> 01:12:19,840
And I think there's so many unexplored of these.

797
01:12:20,400 --> 01:12:22,000
of this niche that we haven't thought of.

798
01:12:22,240 --> 01:12:23,360
And it's kind of exciting.

799
01:12:23,680 --> 01:12:29,280
But then when we said you think about marketing, probably that marketing is not going to send all your money to Facebook.

800
01:12:29,840 --> 01:12:35,200
You're going to have to be a bit more creative because that niche has a particular community that goes with it.

801
01:12:35,840 --> 01:12:38,000
And this is where you're going to look for it.

802
01:12:38,480 --> 01:12:43,200
It's not going to be as simple as, okay, I'm just going to put my money on Facebook and it's going to come back.

803
01:12:43,680 --> 01:12:47,040
Because the more it goes and the more Facebook and Google works for the bigger guys.

804
01:12:47,280 --> 01:13:04,080
So you're going to have to go specifically to your niche to go to your community and be super active on that Reddit is on that subreddit or be inside and I don't know, recruit the one influencer of that niche and have him as an ambassador or something.

805
01:13:04,480 --> 01:13:15,680
You're going to basically have to embed yourself into the community of that niche, which is a very different kind of marketing than just putting 100 grand on Facebook and see if 200 grands are coming back.

806
01:13:16,480 --> 01:13:22,080
I think for these niche apps, marketing means a totally different thing that it does for the bigger guys.

807
01:13:22,720 --> 01:13:23,120
Absolutely.

808
01:13:23,600 --> 01:13:28,640
So we do need to wrap up, but we can't leave Andy Carvell's question just sitting there.

809
01:13:28,800 --> 01:13:30,080
Andy's a good friend.

810
01:13:30,560 --> 01:13:32,320
So Thomas, I'll let you take this one.

811
01:13:33,440 --> 01:13:42,320
Which apps do you see doing really innovative stuff when it comes to subscriptions, either in terms of paywalls, bundling pricing or something else?

812
01:13:43,280 --> 01:13:55,200
Well, one app that you mentioned, so it comes to mind that is very interesting for me is FishBrain because they're they're they're having this subscription model, but that's just one part.

813
01:13:55,360 --> 01:14:09,360
I mean, first because they're a community, they can't push the paywall too hard, otherwise they're going to churn a free user and they need the network effect, so they can't go too hardcore, but they complemented it with other business models that makes a lot of sense for them.

814
01:14:10,240 --> 01:14:14,560
Of course, if you're an Angler, you're going to need some material, some hardware at some point.

815
01:14:14,880 --> 01:14:20,480
So I think for them it's like extremely natural to go towards hybrid model.

816
01:14:21,280 --> 01:14:23,520
And I see going towards hybrid model.

817
01:14:23,840 --> 01:14:35,280
So in their case it's subscription and e commerce, but it can be other things, it can be subscription and ads, it can be subscription and non-renewables, it can be subscription and offline revenue, maybe your Amazon affiliate or whatever.

818
01:14:36,560 --> 01:14:40,720
I see this as extremely interesting and relatively under tapped at the moment.

819
01:14:41,120 --> 01:14:44,880
Like most subscription apps, they're like, yeah, we have subscription, that's it, that's what we do.

820
01:14:44,960 --> 01:14:51,200
And they don't go much beyond that them, I think we're going to see a lot more of these examples.

821
01:14:51,440 --> 01:14:52,800
So Fishbrand is one.

822
01:14:53,200 --> 01:14:58,320
I'm mentioning a couple of others on my post on the RevenueCat blog.

823
01:14:59,280 --> 01:15:07,600
Another one was Fitness Appswed that has built this kind of brand around their leader.

824
01:15:07,920 --> 01:15:10,800
And so they can sell branded goods around it.

825
01:15:11,440 --> 01:15:20,960
And of course, you can put a totally different markup when you already have the audience hooked and convinced toward your brand that you would sell a random object.

826
01:15:21,440 --> 01:15:29,360
So basically my answer here is I'm seeing relatively little innovation in terms of it's not how you design your payroll or the pricing or you made that trick.

827
01:15:29,760 --> 01:15:40,960
Plus, you have the tools that Apple gives you and there are ways to bring them creatively, but it's mostly for me complementing subscription with other modules that is the most innovative.

828
01:15:41,600 --> 01:15:43,840
Well, I think that's a great great way to wrap up.

829
01:15:44,000 --> 01:15:59,040
We talked about from the smallest apps of how you potentially make it go in 2022 to don't think you're going to make a lot of money on merch as a small app, but for the larger apps are finding a lot of innovation in diversifying.

830
01:15:59,840 --> 01:16:01,920
So yeah, if you're a small app, don't have six or seven apps.

831
01:16:01,920 --> 01:16:06,240
app don't have six years of subscription and non-renewables and all this kind of stuff.

832
01:16:06,560 --> 01:16:10,640
But as apps grow, there are opportunities to really diversify revenue.

833
01:16:11,120 --> 01:16:26,000
And I do think that's something we're going to see a lot of in 2022 as experimentation, diversification and innovation in broadening the monetization beyond just that $999 a month subscription.

834
01:16:26,560 --> 01:16:28,080
So yeah, with that, wrap it up.

835
01:16:28,240 --> 01:16:28,640
Thank you, Thomas.

836
